        "title": "07 - Immunomodulators",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "**Systems Bridge:** The immune system is a distributed autonomous agent network running a continuous classification problem: self vs non-self. Tolerance is the allowlisting mechanism: central tolerance (thymic deletion of autoreactive clones) is compile-time allowlist construction; peripheral tolerance (anergy, Treg suppression) is runtime enforcement. Autoimmunity is a false-positive failure mode — the classifier incorrectly marks self-antigens as foreign and activates effectors against them. Immunosuppressive therapy is access control policy modification: broad immunosuppression (corticosteroids, azathioprine, calcineurin inhibitors) is the equivalent of tightening the firewall rules globally — everything gets through less, including the pathological and the beneficial. Targeted biologics (anti-TNF, anti-IL-6, anti-IL-17) are surgical policy changes: block specific signaling molecules that are driving the pathological response while leaving the rest of the immune architecture intact. JAK inhibitors (tofacitinib, baricitinib) block the intracellular signal transduction step downstream of multiple cytokine receptors simultaneously — they are not receptor-specific, but they are pathway-specific, blocking the JAK-STAT signaling node that many cytokines converge on. Transplant immunosuppression is access control configured to tolerate a specific authorized foreign entity (the donor organ) while maintaining maximal defense against true threats — an impossible constraint in principle, which is why opportunistic infections are the primary complication."
    }
